               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origDate when-custom="10XX" notBefore="1000" notAfter="1099"
                        calendar="#gregorian">s. xi</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance type="#l" cert="low" source="1848"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_165"
                        >Crowland, Lincolnshire. Benedictine abbey of St Mary the Virgin, St
                        Bartholomew, and St Guthlac</orgName><p>Originated at Crowland. Provenance
                        inferred from liturgical details. For the Crowland attribution, see Atkins,
                        ‘An investigation of two Anglo-Saxon kalendars’ <title>Archaeologia</title>
                        78 (1928), p. 222, no. 5: 'the presence of two notable Crowland feasts, that
                        of St. Pega (8 Jan.), and the Translation of St. Guthlac (30 Aug.), neither
                        of which is found in any other kalendar, shows conclusively that it is a
                        kalendar of Crowland.'</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#c" cert="low" source="3589"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_310"
                        >Lewes, Sussex. Cluniac priory of St Pancras</orgName><p>Obits of the 12th
                        century suggest that the manuscript was later at Lewes.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName key="org_190" role="fmo">Ely,
                        Cambridgeshire, Benedictine abbey and (1109) cathedral priory of St Peter
                        and St Etheldreda</orgName><p>This attribution was rejected in the second
                        edition of Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964), which did not change
                        in the Supplement (1987). For the original ascription, see the Summary
                        Catalogue IV, p. 584: 'I suggest that the MS was written at or for Ely ...
                        E.W.B.N'. This suggestion was withdrawn in the Summary Catalogue V
                        Corrigenda, p. xxii.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName key="org_2" role="fmo">Peterborough,
                        Northamptonshire, Benedictine abbey of St Peter, St Paul, and St
                        Andrew</orgName><p>This attribution was rejected in the second edition of
                        Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964), which did not change in the
                        Supplement (1987). For the original ascription, see the Summary Catalogue V
                        Corrigenda, p. xxii: 'This was certainly written for Peterborough (H.M.B.)'.
                        The manuscript was reassigned to Crowland in Atkins 1928 (cited in full
                        above).</p></provenance>
               </history>
